<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Carpi</h1><p class="st-meta">140 CE – 318 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Free Dacian people east of the Carpathians who raided Roman Dacia and Moesia throughout the 3rd century until Rome resettled them inside the empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-carpi">Carpi</a> lived in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Moldavia</a>, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> and outside Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>, from the 2nd century CE. They are usually identified with the Poienești archaeological culture of farming villages and cremation cemeteries, and are considered a Dacian people.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived in unfortified villages under tribal leaders, farming and herding. In the crisis of the 3rd century they joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> in raids across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcianopolis">Marcianopolis</a> and attacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-arab">Philip the Arab</a> defeated them in 247, and several emperors took the victory title Carpicus.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Aurelian, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> around 295–305, defeated them and moved large numbers into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>. The last mention comes under Constantine around 318, after which the remaining <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-carpi">Carpi</a> merged into the Gothic and Roman worlds.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 140 CE – 318 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V